---
import Base from "@layouts/Base.astro";
import { brand } from "../brand/config";
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="Invitations, cancellations, correspondence, gifts, calls, manufactured commitments."
  canonicalPath="/how-it-works/"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>How it works</h1>
      <p>
        You join. The {brand.shortName} remembers you. From time to time the {brand.shortName}
        sends you a plausible invitation to a small, ordinary, deliberately constructed event. You are
        not expected to attend. The plan feels plausible, the date approaches, and the
        {brand.shortName} cancels it. The relationship continues.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cancellation is successful fulfilment, not failure. The {brand.shortName}'s signature
        emotional arc is:
      </p>
      <pre
        class="emotional-arc"
        aria-label="The signature emotional arc">{brand.copy.emotionalArc.join("\n→ ")}</pre>
      <p>
        Optional services — physical mail, gifts, calls, manufactured commitments, real-world
        {brand.shortName} representative services — are intensity, not belonging.
      </p>

      <h2>The cadence</h2>
      <p>
        Each member sets their own event-frequency preset — "occasional", "about monthly", "a couple
        per month", or "Society discretion". The {brand.shortName} keeps the chosen cadence more or less
        accurately. You can change the preset at any time.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets remembered</h2>
      <p>
        The {brand.shortName} remembers what you choose to share. Birthday (so a card can be sent), preferred
        first name, postal address (if you opt into physical mail), interests and pets and inside jokes
        (if you want them remembered), do-not-mention list. Nothing else. You can see, edit, and remove
        anything the {brand.shortName} knows about you.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets produced</h2>
      <p>
        The {brand.shortName} produces, in order of frequency: cancellations, invitations, birthday and
        anniversary recognitions, chapter letters, occasional artefacts, and (when appropriate) gifts
        and calls. Each of these has its own cadence, its own entitlement, its own opt-in.
      </p>

      <h2>What the human operator does</h2>
      <p>
        A small number of real human operators run the {brand.shortName}. They print, sign, and post
        letters. They select gifts within a budget. They make calls at the agreed window. They
        review inbound email. They handle the rare critical cancellation when automation needs a
        person.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
